import{_ as n,o,c as t,ao as s}from"./chunks/framework.D7TN4pt9.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"What keeps running when the window closes","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"start/running.md","filePath":"start/running.md","lastUpdated":null}'),i={name:"start/running.md"};function a(h,e,d,r,w,l){return o(),t("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s('<h1 id="What-keeps-running-when-the-window-closes" tabindex="-1">What keeps running when the window closes <a class="header-anchor" href="#What-keeps-running-when-the-window-closes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What keeps running when the window closes {#What-keeps-running-when-the-window-closes}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>The window and the sessions on its computer end together: closing the window ends the backend on that computer and every session it runs, agents included. The next window starts with no sessions.</p><h2 id="Closing-the-window" tabindex="-1">Closing the window <a class="header-anchor" href="#Closing-the-window" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Closing the window {#Closing-the-window}&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><p>The window&#39;s close button, or the system&#39;s close, ends everything: the window waits up to 125 seconds for the sessions to end. A window that crashes or is killed counts as closed.</p></li><li><p><code>Ctrl+Q</code>, in any pane but the agent and the Terminal drawer, asks <strong>Keep the daemon and sessions running?</strong> in a dialog in the middle of the window. No is the default. <code>Tab</code> selects Yes, <code>Enter</code> confirms, and <code>Esc</code> cancels and leaves the window open; a click on No or Yes answers, and a click anywhere else cancels. Yes closes the window and leaves the backend and every session running; the next window shows them again. If the window is killed before the backend has answered, everything ends, as with the close button. In the agent pane and the Terminal drawer the program keeps <code>Ctrl+Q</code> (it is flow control, readline&#39;s quoted-insert and vim&#39;s literal insert), so a short hint in the middle of the window says to press it in the file or preview pane instead.</p></li><li><p>While the window closes, a dialog in the middle of the window, and the bottom of the navigation pane, say what it waits for: <code>closing: waiting for this computer&#39;s backend; it ends the sessions only when no other window is open on this computer</code>. After Yes the wait line says instead that the backend is keeping the sessions and that killing the window before it answers ends them when no other window is open. If a session cannot be ended, the window shows how many are still running before it closes. Those sessions stay listed; end them in Sessions mode with <code>Shift+D</code>.</p></li><li><p>On macOS the <code>Ctrl+Q</code> above is <code>⌘Q</code>, which also belongs to the system&#39;s application menu. The menu most likely takes it first and closes the window without the question, which ends this Mac&#39;s sessions as the close button does; this has not been checked on a Mac. Until it is, press <code>⌘Q</code> only when you mean to end them. To keep them, bind the <code>quit</code> action to another chord in <code>~/.config/sot/keybindings.toml</code> (<a href="./../ref/keybindings">Keybindings</a>); whether that chord avoids the menu is also unchecked.</p></li><li><p>If, after Yes, this computer&#39;s backend does not answer the window in time, the window stays open and asks again, saying the keep was not done; the sessions keep running.</p></li><li><p>When the window relaunches itself for an update, the sessions wait one minute for the new window. If none opens, they end as on a close. A relaunch that cannot be done because the backend did not answer in time leaves the window open and says why in a dialog in the middle of the window, which any key dismisses.</p></li></ul><h2 id="Two-windows-on-one-computer" tabindex="-1">Two windows on one computer <a class="header-anchor" href="#Two-windows-on-one-computer" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Two windows on one computer {#Two-windows-on-one-computer}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Sessions end when the last window on the computer closes. Closing one of two leaves everything running for the other.</p><h2 id="Windows-on-other-computers" tabindex="-1">Windows on other computers <a class="header-anchor" href="#Windows-on-other-computers" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Windows on other computers {#Windows-on-other-computers}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A window that reaches a backend on another computer never ends sessions there: they keep running when it closes, and the next window reattaches. Closing the last window on the computer that runs the backend ends every session there, including any that a window on another computer was viewing.</p><p>Every computer that runs a window also runs its own private backend, so a computer that only runs windows still opens sessions of its own, and they end with its last window. The one exception is a first install made with <code>--backend &lt;ssh-alias&gt;</code> before the hub lists this computer: it has no backend of its own, and its window reaches only that remote backend.</p><h2 id="When-closing-will-not-end-sessions" tabindex="-1">When closing will not end sessions <a class="header-anchor" href="#When-closing-will-not-end-sessions" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;When closing will not end sessions {#When-closing-will-not-end-sessions}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A line at the bottom of the navigation pane says so when this window holds no claim on this computer&#39;s backend: the backend could not verify the window, the backend refused the window&#39;s identity claim, the backend is older than the window, or there is no backend on this computer. Closing such a window leaves every session running.</p><h2 id="At-boot" tabindex="-1">At boot <a class="header-anchor" href="#At-boot" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;At boot {#At-boot}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>On a Linux standard install the backend starts at boot as a user service. Elsewhere, opening the window starts it.</p>',13)])])}const u=n(i,[["render",a]]);export{p as __pageData,u as default};
